WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Symbolic-link risk often appears during creation and replacement.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:11.960
If code checks for a filename, then later creates it, the object at that path may not be the object the earlier check described.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.500
Safer workflows use platform-appropriate creation behavior that creates and opens the intended file atomically.

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.300
Temporary files overlap strongly with symbolic-link safety.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:29.160
Temporary names should not be predictable, and temporary directories should be selected according to trust, permissions, and data sensitivity.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.780
The program should own the returned descriptor or handle and should not rely only on a generated pathname.

7
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.780
Replacement workflows should be intentional.

8
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.200
A program may write to a working file and later publish final output.

9
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:52.460
The code should distinguish working files from final files, check write results, handle partial files, and expose final content only after the operation is complete and validated.

10
00:00:52.740 --> 00:01:02.900
Rename-style finalization or other durable replacement patterns can be useful at a high level, but the exact guarantees depend on platform and filesystem behavior.

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.160
The review should focus on whether final output appears only after the program has completed and checked the work.

12
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.920
Cleanup also belongs in the creation story.

13
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.700
If creation succeeds but later validation fails, the program should know who removes the working file.

14
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.680
If replacement fails, the program should avoid leaving ambiguous state.

15
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.380
Safe creation is only complete when finalization and cleanup behavior are clear.
